messages.content:
{
  "explanation": "I can help you fix MMS. MMS needs working cellular service, mobile data, correct APN/MMSC settings, and a few phone settings to be just right. I’ll identify your line to check for account/plan issues, while you run a few quick checks on the phone.",
  "identity_request": "To locate your line and rule out any account or data-cap issues, please provide ONE of the following: (a) the 10-digit phone number of the affected line, (b) your customer ID, or (c) your full name and date of birth (YYYY-MM-DD).",
  "questions": [
    "Do regular texts (SMS) and calls work right now?",
    "Are you currently traveling outside your usual area or abroad?",
    "Do you see any error message when an MMS fails? If yes, what does it say?"
  ],
  "device_steps": [
    {
      "step": "Check the status bar for basics",
      "how": "Make sure Airplane Mode is OFF. Confirm you see signal bars and a network type like 4G/LTE/5G (not 2G/E). If you’re on Wi‑Fi, that’s fine—we’ll turn it off briefly in the next step.",
      "reason": "MMS needs cellular service and a modern network type."
    },
    {
      "step": "Verify mobile data works",
      "how": "Temporarily turn Wi‑Fi OFF. Ensure Mobile Data is ON. Then try loading a web page in your browser.",
      "reason": "If mobile data isn’t working, MMS won’t send. We’ll fix data first if needed."
    },
    {
      "step": "If you are traveling",
      "how": "On the phone, ensure Data Roaming is ON. Tell me you’re traveling so I can also check/enable roaming on your line if needed.",
      "reason": "MMS over roaming requires phone-side roaming ON and line-side roaming enabled."
    },
    {
      "step": "Test MMS quickly",
      "how": "With Wi‑Fi still OFF and mobile data ON, send a photo to yourself. Note any error or behavior (e.g., “sending…”, “failed”, or “stuck”).",
      "reason": "Confirms whether MMS can initiate over cellular data."
    },
    {
      "step": "Turn off Wi‑Fi Calling (temporarily)",
      "how": "If Wi‑Fi Calling is enabled, turn it OFF and try sending the MMS again.",
      "reason": "Some configurations interfere with MMS routing."
    },
    {
      "step": "Check messaging app permissions",
      "how": "In Settings > Apps > Messaging (your default SMS app) > Permissions, make sure Storage/Photos and SMS permissions are allowed.",
      "reason": "Without media/SMS permissions, MMS may fail."
    },
    {
      "step": "Check APN/MMSC quickly",
      "how": "In Mobile Network > Access Point Names, open your carrier APN and see if an MMSC URL is present. If you’re unsure or it looks blank/wrong, use ‘Reset to default APN’ and then reboot the phone.",
      "reason": "A missing/incorrect MMSC is a very common MMS failure cause."
    }
  ],
  "what_to_reply_with": "Please share: (1) your identification info (one of the options above), (2) whether SMS/calls work, (3) whether you’re traveling, and (4) the results of the steps—especially whether mobile data works with Wi‑Fi OFF, your network type (e.g., 4G/5G), and what happens when you try to send an MMS."
}

